00:00:24.154 --> 00:00:25.646
- [Beaver] Harlan County, Kentucky.

00:00:25.646 --> 00:00:26.479
- [Interviewer] Harlan County.

00:00:26.479 --> 00:00:28.659
And tell me about your childhood.

00:00:28.659 --> 00:00:29.849
You had both your parents
when you were a kid?

00:00:29.849 --> 00:00:32.198
- Both my parents, yeah.

00:00:32.198 --> 00:00:34.546
- [Interviewer] What kind
of childhood do you have?

00:00:34.546 --> 00:00:37.752
- Well, in my family I'm eighth.

00:00:37.752 --> 00:00:40.366
There are twelve of us, I
was the eighth young one.

00:00:40.366 --> 00:00:42.208
I had a lot of hand me downs,

00:00:42.208 --> 00:00:43.939
but I had a pretty decent life.

00:00:43.939 --> 00:00:45.724
- [Interviewer] Yeah, this
is a poor part of the country

00:00:45.724 --> 00:00:47.631
and your family was probably poor.

00:00:47.631 --> 00:00:48.464
- My dad had to go out to work.

00:00:48.464 --> 00:00:50.185
He worked out of state.

00:00:50.185 --> 00:00:51.085
- [Interviewer] What did your dad do?

00:00:51.085 --> 00:00:52.343
- He did carpentry work,
Carpenter.

00:00:52.343 --> 00:00:54.284
- He built tables, (indistinct) tables.

00:00:54.284 --> 00:00:55.744
- [Interviewer] And how was your mom?

00:00:55.744 --> 00:00:57.694
- My mom, she was a housewife.

00:00:57.694 --> 00:01:00.065
You know, she raised the garden.

00:01:00.065 --> 00:01:02.846
Taught us what we know,
what we really know.

00:01:02.846 --> 00:01:05.724
Dad, he was gone five
days out of the week.

00:01:05.724 --> 00:01:07.857
Come home on weekend.

00:01:07.857 --> 00:01:09.455
And then he'd stay drunk all weekend.

00:01:09.455 --> 00:01:10.822
- [Interviewer] Then you
left home at what age?

00:01:10.822 --> 00:01:12.885
- 13, I was 13 when I left at home.

00:01:12.885 --> 00:01:15.081
- [Interviewer] What kind of kid were you?

00:01:15.081 --> 00:01:16.778
- Hard to handle.

00:01:16.778 --> 00:01:19.513
My old man whooped me all the time.

00:01:19.513 --> 00:01:21.158
I hear him come out hollering, I'd leave.

00:01:21.158 --> 00:01:22.477
I'd hit the mountain.

00:01:22.477 --> 00:01:23.470
My mom would wake me up.

00:01:23.470 --> 00:01:24.880
- [Interviewer] So you left home at 13?

00:01:24.880 --> 00:01:25.910
- At 13, yeah.

00:01:25.910 --> 00:01:27.728
- [Interviewer] And went up to where？

00:01:27.728 --> 00:01:29.995
- Oh, I slept in barns, people's garages,

00:01:29.995 --> 00:01:32.626
buildings, basement, smokehouses.

00:01:32.626 --> 00:01:33.884
- [Interviewer] You finished high school?

00:01:33.884 --> 00:01:34.717
- Huh?

00:01:34.717 --> 00:01:35.558
- [Interviewer] Did you go to high school?

00:01:35.558 --> 00:01:37.076
- I went to about a year in high school.

00:01:37.076 --> 00:01:39.245
- [Interviewer] Yeah.

00:01:39.245 --> 00:01:41.245
- It didn't interest me.

00:01:42.286 --> 00:01:44.065
I wanted something else, something better.

00:01:44.065 --> 00:01:45.005
- [Interviewer] What kind of things

00:01:45.005 --> 00:01:47.066
have you done for work in your life?

00:01:47.066 --> 00:01:49.585
- Well, carpentry, I've
built couple houses.

00:01:49.585 --> 00:01:51.601
You know, like lay block and brick,

00:01:51.601 --> 00:01:55.584
rock, carpentry work. (indistinct)

00:01:55.584 --> 00:01:58.961
I have learned that
from my day and brother.

00:01:58.961 --> 00:02:00.880
- [Interviewer] Right.
And you make Moonshine?

00:02:00.880 --> 00:02:02.235
- Yes, I make moonshine, yes.

00:02:02.235 --> 00:02:05.557
- [Interviewer] And
tell me about moonshine.

00:02:05.557 --> 00:02:07.231
How do you make moonshine?

00:02:07.231 --> 00:02:08.543
- Well, you gotta make beer first.

00:02:08.543 --> 00:02:11.832
You gotta put malt, malt and corn.

00:02:11.832 --> 00:02:14.549
Then once it sprouts,
you got to ground it up.

00:02:14.549 --> 00:02:18.490
And then you gotta put
it in a barrel of water.

00:02:18.490 --> 00:02:20.688
Then you add water,
then you add your yeast,

00:02:20.688 --> 00:02:23.290
then you add your meal,
then you add your sugar.

00:02:23.290 --> 00:02:26.110
The more sugar you add,
the more liquor you got.

00:02:26.110 --> 00:02:27.981
So you got a 50 pound gallon drum,

00:02:27.981 --> 00:02:30.705
you put 50 pound of sugar.

00:02:30.705 --> 00:02:33.486
Then you wait and it germinates.

00:02:33.486 --> 00:02:37.734
Then you taste it every couple
days and it turns into beer.

00:02:37.734 --> 00:02:39.514
And then you put it in a still.

00:02:39.514 --> 00:02:41.755
Then you run it through a copper worm.

00:02:41.755 --> 00:02:43.083
(indistinct)

00:02:43.083 --> 00:02:46.963
Then you run your worm in cold water.

00:02:46.963 --> 00:02:48.751
And once that steam hits that cold water,

00:02:48.751 --> 00:02:50.620
it just turns into alcohol,

00:02:50.620 --> 00:02:53.277
and you drop it in a jug, and a jar.

00:02:53.277 --> 00:02:54.780
- [Interviewer] It ends
up clear as water, right?

00:02:54.780 --> 00:02:56.993
- Clear as water, yeah.

00:02:56.993 --> 00:02:57.918
- [Interviewer] There's
a bottle of it right

00:02:57.918 --> 00:02:58.751
in front of you.

00:02:58.751 --> 00:03:00.657
What is moonshine like? Is it like Vodka?

00:03:00.657 --> 00:03:01.626
Is it like-

00:03:01.626 --> 00:03:03.211
- Well, it is got its own taste.

00:03:03.211 --> 00:03:05.175
- [Interviewer] It is harder
than Whiskey or Vodka, right?

00:03:05.175 --> 00:03:08.688
- It's more like Bourbon
than anything else.

00:03:08.688 --> 00:03:10.963
Comes out of the still about 140 proof.

00:03:10.963 --> 00:03:11.941
- [Interviewer] 140 proof.

00:03:11.941 --> 00:03:14.117
- Then you gotta take good clear water

00:03:14.117 --> 00:03:15.763
to tamper it down to where you want it.

00:03:15.763 --> 00:03:17.740
80 proof or 90 proof, or 100 proof.

00:03:17.740 --> 00:03:19.946
My old man only drank 100 proof liquor.

00:03:19.946 --> 00:03:22.323
Drink anything under 100
proof won't hurt you.

00:03:22.323 --> 00:03:23.281
That's what he told me.

00:03:23.281 --> 00:03:24.114
- [Interviewer] And how do you drink it?

00:03:24.114 --> 00:03:24.957
You don't drink it, right?

00:03:24.957 --> 00:03:26.229
- Yeah, you sip on it.

00:03:26.229 --> 00:03:27.207
- [Interviewer] You just
put your lips to it really-

00:03:27.207 --> 00:03:28.065
- Right.

00:03:28.065 --> 00:03:30.305
You drink quick, you will be drunk quick.

00:03:30.305 --> 00:03:32.025
(Interviewer laughing)

00:03:32.025 --> 00:03:34.594
It will knock you on
your ass pretty quick.

00:03:34.594 --> 00:03:35.676
- [Interviewer] And
Moonshine is popular here

00:03:35.676 --> 00:03:36.608
in this part of country?

00:03:36.608 --> 00:03:37.990
- Yes, it is.

00:03:37.990 --> 00:03:39.042
- [Interviewer] Yeah, lots of
people we've talked to said,

00:03:39.042 --> 00:03:40.436
"Can you get me any Moonshine?"

00:03:40.436 --> 00:03:41.269
- Yeah.

00:03:41.269 --> 00:03:42.102
- [Interviewer] Told them
I'm gonna talk to you,

00:03:42.102 --> 00:03:43.393
and they said, "Yeah, get me some."

00:03:43.393 --> 00:03:44.896
- It is pretty easy to find.

00:03:44.896 --> 00:03:46.046
- [Interviewer] Yeah.

00:03:46.046 --> 00:03:47.711
- There's still a couple
people here you will find it.

00:03:47.711 --> 00:03:48.578
- [Interviewer] It's illegal?

00:03:48.578 --> 00:03:51.031
- It's illegal, yeah.
Yeah.

00:03:51.031 --> 00:03:53.710
What are the penalties
like for making Moonshine?

00:03:53.710 --> 00:03:56.491
- Well, it's a federal offense.

00:03:56.491 --> 00:03:57.696
You're allowed to have a still,

00:03:57.696 --> 00:04:00.613
but you can't have it put together.

00:04:02.164 --> 00:04:03.335
- [Interviewer] What was
the purpose of having

00:04:03.335 --> 00:04:05.755
a drink that's so strong?

00:04:05.755 --> 00:04:08.150
- Well, they say the stronger the better.

00:04:08.150 --> 00:04:10.965
I mean it kills all the
worms in you, you know.

00:04:10.965 --> 00:04:13.339
They say that cheap
liquor, you know, rot guts.

00:04:13.339 --> 00:04:14.395
- [Interviewer] Right.

00:04:14.395 --> 00:04:16.031
- It hurts you.

00:04:16.031 --> 00:04:17.909
Normally I only drink a 100 proof liquor.

00:04:17.909 --> 00:04:20.234
- [Interviewer] Yeah,
your dad used to drink it?

00:04:20.234 --> 00:04:22.089
- He drank it all the time,

00:04:22.089 --> 00:04:23.169
when he weren't working.

00:04:23.169 --> 00:04:24.869
- [Interviewer] Yeah,
tell me about your life.

00:04:24.869 --> 00:04:27.651
So you, you got married at one point?

00:04:27.651 --> 00:04:29.807
- Yeah, I got married in '90.

00:04:29.807 --> 00:04:32.050
We were together twenty
something years before she died,

00:04:32.050 --> 00:04:33.947
twenty-one years really.

00:04:33.947 --> 00:04:36.697
Until she died of cancer in 2011.

00:04:38.859 --> 00:04:40.397
- [Interviewer] And you have a daughter?

00:04:40.397 --> 00:04:43.900
- I have one daughter,
yeah, she was born in 2014.

00:04:43.900 --> 00:04:45.909
- [Interviewer] Since your-

00:04:45.909 --> 00:04:47.556
- Since my woman died, yeah.

00:04:47.556 --> 00:04:50.393
She got me over her.
- Yeah.

00:04:50.393 --> 00:04:53.921
Then this girl, that was
one wish that come true.

00:04:53.921 --> 00:04:55.689
I always wished I had a
young one before I died.

00:04:55.689 --> 00:04:58.185
I raised three step young ones you know.

00:04:58.185 --> 00:05:01.833
Her boy was a year and a
half when we got together.

00:05:01.833 --> 00:05:04.548
Now, I walked both my
girls down the aisle.

00:05:04.548 --> 00:05:07.348
Their real dads are still living.

00:05:07.348 --> 00:05:09.103
That's the only time I
wore a tux in my life

00:05:09.103 --> 00:05:10.159
for going to a wedding.

00:05:10.159 --> 00:05:12.345
- [Interviewer] Yeah, tell me
about living in Appalachia.

00:05:12.345 --> 00:05:14.426
It's a one of the poorest
parts of the country.

00:05:14.426 --> 00:05:16.142
- It's hard to get a dollar, you know?

00:05:16.142 --> 00:05:17.277
- [Interviewer] It's
hard to make money here,

00:05:17.277 --> 00:05:18.124
