- It is very hard.

00:05:18.124 --> 00:05:21.235
- Unless you got garden
work or odd jobs for people.

00:05:21.235 --> 00:05:22.086
- [Interviewer] And coal mining

00:05:22.086 --> 00:05:24.219
which used to be the only
option is fading away.

00:05:24.219 --> 00:05:28.414
- Yeah, I never did like coal
mining because it's dangerous.

00:05:28.414 --> 00:05:30.097
Or logging, I never did
nothing like logging

00:05:30.097 --> 00:05:31.081
because it's dangerous.

00:05:31.081 --> 00:05:32.831
- [Interviewer] Yeah.

00:05:34.632 --> 00:05:36.448
Do you consider yourself a hillbilly?

00:05:36.448 --> 00:05:38.146
- Yes, 100 %.

00:05:38.146 --> 00:05:39.405
- [Interviewer] Tell me about
it, what is a hillbilly?

00:05:39.405 --> 00:05:40.731
Is it a derogatory term?

00:05:40.731 --> 00:05:41.840
Is it a negative term?

00:05:41.840 --> 00:05:44.933
- Well, a hillbilly is just
born and raised on the land.

00:05:44.933 --> 00:05:46.855
You live off the land if you wanted to.

00:05:46.855 --> 00:05:48.083
- [Interviewer] You are
proud of being a hillbilly?

00:05:48.083 --> 00:05:49.192
- I'm proud of it, yeah.

00:05:49.192 --> 00:05:50.454
I wouldn't take nothing else,

00:05:50.454 --> 00:05:51.299
I wouldn't be nothing else.

00:05:51.299 --> 00:05:52.776
- [Interviewer] What is a hillbilly?

00:05:52.776 --> 00:05:55.486
Tell me, what's your
definition of hillbilly?

00:05:55.486 --> 00:05:58.139
- Live on your own. Live off the land.

00:05:58.139 --> 00:06:01.760
You know, take pride in everything you do.

00:06:01.760 --> 00:06:03.588
And you know, that's about it.

00:06:03.588 --> 00:06:04.732
- [Interviewer] It's
almost like an outlaw?

00:06:04.732 --> 00:06:05.700
- Yeah, I been an outlaw.

00:06:05.700 --> 00:06:06.701
- [Interviewer] For this
part of the country.

00:06:06.701 --> 00:06:07.973
- Outlaw from the law, yeah.

00:06:07.973 --> 00:06:09.845
But I've had my share of jail time.

00:06:09.845 --> 00:06:10.678
- [Interviewer] Have you?

00:06:10.678 --> 00:06:11.511
- Yeah.

00:06:11.511 --> 00:06:13.178
- [Interviewer] What kind
of stuff got you in jail?

00:06:13.178 --> 00:06:14.030
- Drugs.

00:06:14.030 --> 00:06:15.681
- [Interviewer] Drugs?
You used to use drugs?

00:06:15.681 --> 00:06:16.990
- Yeah, I do drugs, yeah.

00:06:16.990 --> 00:06:17.951
- [Interviewer] You still do?

00:06:17.951 --> 00:06:18.995
- Once in a while.

00:06:18.995 --> 00:06:21.526
- [Interviewer] But what have
you played with in the past?

00:06:21.526 --> 00:06:25.193
- Cocaine, heroin, pills and marijuana.

00:06:25.193 --> 00:06:27.330
I still smoke Marijuana once in a while.

00:06:27.330 --> 00:06:28.967
- [Interviewer] I think the
first time I photographed you

00:06:28.967 --> 00:06:30.307
you had a whole field of Marijuana.

00:06:30.307 --> 00:06:32.630
Didn't you?
- Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:06:32.630 --> 00:06:34.374
That was 12 years ago.
(Interviewer laughs)

00:06:34.374 --> 00:06:37.085
- [Interviewer] Yeah,
and living here, I mean,

00:06:37.085 --> 00:06:38.922
what are the upsides and
downsides of Appalachia?

00:06:38.922 --> 00:06:40.832
I mean the doctors and
dentists are very few

00:06:40.832 --> 00:06:41.853
and far between, right?

00:06:41.853 --> 00:06:43.995
- Yeah, it's hard to pay bills

00:06:43.995 --> 00:06:45.403
if you've ain't got no income.

00:06:45.403 --> 00:06:49.051
I draw SSI now, though,
but 'cause of my health.

00:06:49.051 --> 00:06:50.093
- [Interviewer] Right,
how old are you now?

00:06:50.093 --> 00:06:51.674
- I'm 58.
58.

00:06:51.674 --> 00:06:52.507
- Yeah.

00:06:53.671 --> 00:06:54.566
- [Interviewer] Have you traveled?

00:06:54.566 --> 00:06:55.846
Have you been in other
parts of the country?

00:06:55.846 --> 00:06:59.336
- Yeah, I've been to Chicago
and Florida, Georgia.

00:06:59.336 --> 00:07:01.164
You know, places like that.

00:07:01.164 --> 00:07:02.746
But I didn't like the city life.

00:07:02.746 --> 00:07:04.336
I stayed in Chicago five years.

00:07:04.336 --> 00:07:05.897
I worked up there, but,

00:07:05.897 --> 00:07:07.578
I made big money and spent big money.

00:07:07.578 --> 00:07:08.642
But there ain't no place like home.

00:07:08.642 --> 00:07:09.475
- [Interviewer] This is one of the most

00:07:09.475 --> 00:07:10.485
beautiful parts of the country.

00:07:10.485 --> 00:07:11.318
- It is, it is.

00:07:11.318 --> 00:07:12.151
- [Interviewer] It might
be one of the poorest

00:07:12.151 --> 00:07:13.305
and toughest to survive-

00:07:13.305 --> 00:07:14.138
- Yeah it is.

00:07:14.138 --> 00:07:15.448
- [Interviewer] But it's
spectacularly beautiful.

00:07:15.448 --> 00:07:16.690
- Yes, it is.

00:07:16.690 --> 00:07:17.523
- [Interviewer] Are you happy?

00:07:17.523 --> 00:07:19.383
- I'm happy here, yeah, I sure am.

00:07:19.383 --> 00:07:20.782
- [Interviewer] Yeah,
you always seem to be.

00:07:20.782 --> 00:07:21.952
I photographed you for my first book,

00:07:21.952 --> 00:07:22.785
and you seemed to be-

00:07:22.785 --> 00:07:24.158
- Yeah, yeah, yeah, sure enough.

00:07:24.158 --> 00:07:25.256
- [Interviewer] Truly loving this life.

00:07:25.256 --> 00:07:26.239
- Yeah.

00:07:26.239 --> 00:07:30.613
- [Interviewer] What are your
best memories in your life?

00:07:30.613 --> 00:07:33.797
- Best memories, I guess
being with my family.

00:07:33.797 --> 00:07:35.340
Having family reunions.

00:07:35.340 --> 00:07:37.361
But I don't have that no more.

00:07:37.361 --> 00:07:39.296
Every time we get together
now we always fight,

00:07:39.296 --> 00:07:41.713
we wanna fight all the time.

00:07:41.713 --> 00:07:44.130
(indistinct)

00:07:46.799 --> 00:07:48.393
- [Interviewer] Tell me
more about Appalachia.

00:07:48.393 --> 00:07:49.519
There's hunting here?

00:07:49.519 --> 00:07:50.352
There's a lot of animals, right?

00:07:50.352 --> 00:07:51.766
- Yeah, a lot of animals, you know,

00:07:51.766 --> 00:07:54.266
bear, hunt bear, turkey, deer.

00:07:55.683 --> 00:07:57.925
But my woman she wouldn't
fix nothing like it.

00:07:57.925 --> 00:07:59.939
She wanted everything
bought out of a store.

00:07:59.939 --> 00:08:02.842
I wouldn't kill it
unless she wanna eat it.

00:08:02.842 --> 00:08:06.754
Me, I can eat anything like
that, but she wasn't like it.

00:08:06.754 --> 00:08:11.578
If it wasn't bought from a
store she wouldn't eat it.

00:08:11.578 --> 00:08:12.571
- [Interviewer] What is
the most important lesson

00:08:12.571 --> 00:08:14.359
you've learned in your life?

00:08:14.359 --> 00:08:17.147
- Be honest, be honest, be honest.

00:08:17.147 --> 00:08:18.945
Honesty is the best policy.

00:08:18.945 --> 00:08:21.232
I learned that the hard way.

00:08:21.232 --> 00:08:25.223
I stole stuff in my life, it don't pay.

00:08:25.223 --> 00:08:26.642
It does not pay.

00:08:26.642 --> 00:08:28.653
Honesty is the best policy.

00:08:28.653 --> 00:08:31.381
It is hard too around this
neighborhood, you know?

00:08:31.381 --> 00:08:36.152
It feels good to get a
lick up, though, it does.

00:08:36.152 --> 00:08:38.761
And I've done it 20
years, and no retirement.

00:08:38.761 --> 00:08:41.496
But I had to make some kind of living.

00:08:41.496 --> 00:08:42.782
- [Interviewer] Yeah.

00:08:42.782 --> 00:08:45.164
- It's nothin' to brag about.

00:08:45.164 --> 00:08:47.601
- [Interviewer] What regrets
do you have in your life?

00:08:47.601 --> 00:08:48.434
- Pardon?

00:08:48.434 --> 00:08:49.714
- [Interviewer] Do you have any regrets?

00:08:49.714 --> 00:08:52.284
- Yeah, a whole lot of regrets.

00:08:52.284 --> 00:08:54.408
I stole off my family, this and that,

00:08:54.408 --> 00:08:56.542
to be able to build up my.

00:08:56.542 --> 00:08:58.602
Took me 40 years to realize, you know,

00:08:58.602 --> 00:09:01.268
what was wrong with my life.

00:09:01.268 --> 00:09:03.927
I came back here by myself.

00:09:03.927 --> 00:09:05.823
I'm better off that way I guess, you know.

00:09:05.823 --> 00:09:08.089
I don't have to worry about
nobody else bother me.

00:09:08.089 --> 00:09:11.569
I aint gonna bother nobody else.

00:09:11.569 --> 00:09:12.689
- [Interviewer] How is your personality

00:09:12.689 --> 00:09:15.409
different than everyone
else in your family?

00:09:15.409 --> 00:09:19.397
- Well, I'm the last boy that was born,

00:09:19.397 --> 00:09:22.411
and I got three fingers, I guess.

00:09:22.411 --> 00:09:24.587
I'm just the oddball out of the bunch,

00:09:24.587 --> 00:09:27.067
I am the only one who
is never had a real job.

00:09:27.067 --> 00:09:28.309
I always survived on my own,

00:09:28.309 --> 00:09:31.921
picked up pop cans and
bottles for a living.

00:09:31.921 --> 00:09:34.421
Just try to live off the land.

00:09:36.050 --> 00:09:38.717
You know, my hat it's all I got.

00:09:39.698 --> 00:09:42.408
It's all I got here.
Everything is mine out here.

00:09:42.408 --> 00:09:44.138
I've got stuff people give to me,

00:09:44.138 --> 00:09:46.219
and I've got out of garbage
dumps and this and that.

00:09:46.219 --> 00:09:48.117
- [Interviewer] So you live
up on top of a mountaintop

00:09:48.117 --> 00:09:49.450
all by yourself here.
- Yeah, yeah.

00:09:49.450 --> 00:09:50.699
I don't think the cops could even find you

00:09:50.699 --> 00:09:51.828
if they wanted to.

00:09:51.828 --> 00:09:53.285
- They've been here a time or two.

00:09:53.285 --> 00:09:55.337
They've been here, they've been here.

00:09:55.337 --> 00:09:56.547
- [Interviewer] Everyone
in this town knows you?

00:09:56.547 --> 00:10:00.215
- Yeah, I don't know
them, but they know me.

00:10:00.215 --> 00:10:04.226
Yeah, a lot of people know
me, but I don't know them,

00:10:04.226 --> 00:10:05.893
in different states.

00:10:06.996 --> 00:10:11.996
- [Interviewer] Yeah, what
are you famous for？(laughs)

00:10:12.076 --> 00:10:12.972
- Growing pot, I guess.

00:10:12.972 --> 00:10:13.805
- [Interviewer] Moonshine and pot?

00:10:13.805 --> 00:10:16.358
- Moonshine and pot, yeah.

00:10:16.358 --> 00:10:17.930
- [Interviewer] So tell
me about your life,

00:10:17.930 --> 00:10:21.082
you still have hope for
things in your life or?

00:10:21.082 --> 00:10:24.553
- I'd like to see my
girl's eighteenth birthday.

00:10:24.553 --> 00:10:28.401
That's the only thing
I hope in my life now.

00:10:28.401 --> 00:10:29.560
That's 11 more years.

00:10:29.560 --> 00:10:31.282
I don't know if I'll
make 11 more years or not

00:10:31.282 --> 00:10:33.282
if I don't quit my ways.

00:10:36.210 --> 00:10:37.850
She'll be seven in May.

00:10:37.850 --> 00:10:39.034
- [Interviewer] Yeah.

00:10:39.034 --> 00:10:42.284
- I was 52 years old when she was born.

00:10:43.393 --> 00:10:44.890
- [Interviewer] Did you have friends here?

00:10:44.890 --> 00:10:46.159
- Yeah, I got all kinds of friends, yeah,

00:10:46.159 --> 00:10:48.169
Well, so called friends, you know.

00:10:48.169 --> 00:10:49.302
When you're down and
out that's when you know

00:10:49.302 --> 00:10:51.513
who your friends are, or you're in jail.

00:10:51.513 --> 00:10:53.142
- [Interviewer] Tell me about your hand.

00:10:53.142 --> 00:10:54.100
What happened to your hand?

00:10:54.100 --> 00:10:56.500
- Well, that feller right
there, Edward Caldwell,

00:10:56.500 --> 00:10:58.366
we were chopping wood in '74.

00:10:58.366 --> 00:11:00.565
(indistinct) My old man
played cards all night,

00:11:00.565 --> 00:11:02.591
come in and wanted to build a fire.

00:11:02.591 --> 00:11:04.231
And we went outside in the dark at about

00:11:04.231 --> 00:11:05.912
one o'clock in the morning.

00:11:05.912 --> 00:11:08.648
And we had a pile of kindling
on a big ol' chop block.

00:11:08.648 --> 00:11:12.526
And I laid up a piece of
wood about six inches round.

00:11:12.526 --> 00:11:14.453
The chop came and he was
coming down with the axe

00:11:14.453 --> 00:11:17.002
and cut (indistinct) I
got it's head back up.

00:11:17.002 --> 00:11:18.643
That's when he come down
with the axe and cut it off.

00:11:18.643 --> 00:11:21.539
Cut this one, (indistinct).

00:11:21.539 --> 00:11:23.582
And I raised it up like that.

00:11:23.582 --> 00:11:25.353
I clapped my hands together
like that and passed out.

00:11:25.353 --> 00:11:27.198
They ran me over to the hospital.

00:11:27.198 --> 00:11:28.690
- [Interviewer] So show me
your hand you're missing-

00:11:28.690 --> 00:11:30.733
- My little pinky.

00:11:30.733 --> 00:11:33.606
And I remember I had
(indistinct) in winter time

00:11:33.606 --> 00:11:35.736
and I had two or three sweatshirts on

00:11:35.736 --> 00:11:37.324
and I remember cutting up my arm.

00:11:37.324 --> 00:11:39.605
And then digging up in my
arm to get it by itself

00:11:39.605 --> 00:11:41.582
and blood everywhere.

00:11:41.582 --> 00:11:42.415
- [Interviewer] How long does it take to

00:11:42.415 --> 00:11:43.681
get to a hospital here?

00:11:43.681 --> 00:11:44.514
- About two hours.

00:11:44.514 --> 00:11:46.093
They had to give me three pints of blood.

00:11:46.093 --> 00:11:47.697
It happened over here on the other side,

00:11:47.697 --> 00:11:50.512
where the picture was taken
is where I lost my finger at.

00:11:50.512 --> 00:11:51.635
- [Interviewer] Right.

00:11:51.635 --> 00:11:53.066
- But, these days they can put it back on,

00:11:53.066 --> 00:11:54.790
but back in '74 before they didn't.

00:11:54.790 --> 00:11:57.051
- [Interviewer] Yeah,
and he was your friend,

00:11:57.051 --> 00:11:59.460
and he remained your friend
after removing your finger?

00:11:59.460 --> 00:12:02.221
- He's my brother-in-law,
yeah, he dated my sister.

00:12:02.221 --> 00:12:05.529
And it was an accident, he
felt guilty for a long time.

00:12:05.529 --> 00:12:06.834
But you know.

00:12:06.834 --> 00:12:08.051
- [Interviewer] Do you
ever wonder what your life

00:12:08.051 --> 00:12:10.951
would been like if you'd
been born in New York city?

00:12:10.951 --> 00:12:11.784
- Oh, it would have been pitiful.

00:12:11.784 --> 00:12:15.208
I'd probably been dead by now, probably.

00:12:15.208 --> 00:12:17.625
I'd say, but I can't tell it.

00:12:19.130 --> 00:12:22.488
I've had a good life the
way I look at it, you know.

00:12:22.488 --> 00:12:24.128
I was never a hog, I was never greedy.

00:12:24.128 --> 00:12:28.036
I'd take a small amount
and go about my business.

00:12:28.036 --> 00:12:31.863
I never steal nothing
that I didn't really need.

00:12:31.863 --> 00:12:33.505
To be honest with you, I mean,

00:12:33.505 --> 00:12:35.424
when I was in the court system,

00:12:35.424 --> 00:12:37.137
I always told the truth.

00:12:37.137 --> 00:12:39.513
That's what my mom always told me.

00:12:39.513 --> 00:12:40.875
- [Interviewer] So what
do you do for enjoyment

00:12:40.875 --> 00:12:41.972
at this point in your life?

00:12:41.972 --> 00:12:43.972
What makes you happy?

00:12:43.972 --> 00:12:45.063
- Well, I've always had,

00:12:45.063 --> 00:12:46.781
as long as I've got a dollar in my pocket

00:12:46.781 --> 00:12:48.557
Or a joint I'm happy.

00:12:48.557 --> 00:12:52.952
I got something to eat,
and my dog ain't hungry.

00:12:52.952 --> 00:12:55.526
I don't ask nobody for nothing.

00:12:55.526 --> 00:12:58.289
I do that before, I ask somebody.

00:12:58.289 --> 00:12:59.996
It's always been that way.

00:12:59.996 --> 00:13:02.454
If I can't get it from home I do without.

00:13:02.454 --> 00:13:04.621
It's always been that way.

00:13:05.538 --> 00:13:06.371
- [Interviewer] When you were a kid,

00:13:06.371 --> 00:13:07.538
did you have dreams of
doing something in life?

00:13:07.538 --> 00:13:08.371
- Yeah.

00:13:08.371 --> 00:13:09.458
- [Interviewer] What kind of stuff?

00:13:09.458 --> 00:13:10.648
- Being a cop.

00:13:10.648 --> 00:13:11.854
- Being a cop?
- Yeah.

00:13:11.854 --> 00:13:12.723
- [Interviewer] You ended
up the opposite of that.

00:13:12.723 --> 00:13:16.763
- Yeah, I was in the big
pen when I was twelve.

00:13:16.763 --> 00:13:20.576
Grand theft and breaking and entering.

00:13:20.576 --> 00:13:23.554
(indistinct)

00:13:23.554 --> 00:13:24.387
Yup.

00:13:25.482 --> 00:13:28.021
I was 18 years old before
I got out of grade school.

00:13:28.021 --> 00:13:30.114
That was one tough
decision going to school.

00:13:30.114 --> 00:13:32.503
I was 18 before I got out of grade school.

00:13:32.503 --> 00:13:35.977
I graduated from eighth
grade on my 18th birthday.

00:13:35.977 --> 00:13:36.935
- [Interviewer] Really?

00:13:36.935 --> 00:13:40.059
- [Beaver] Yeah, when I graduated yeah.

00:13:40.059 --> 00:13:41.236
Well, you know I wanted to go,

00:13:41.236 --> 00:13:42.717
I had to go to school to
eat and hit the mountain,

00:13:42.717 --> 00:13:46.239
and I missed a hundred or
something days of the year.

00:13:46.239 --> 00:13:48.035
- [Interviewer] You had
to go back to school?

00:13:48.035 --> 00:13:50.319
- Yeah. I went to school to eat, though.

00:13:50.319 --> 00:13:52.955
Twelve of us in the family it was hard

00:13:52.955 --> 00:13:54.579
to raise enough garden, you know.

00:13:54.579 --> 00:13:56.746
We raise 200 pounds of 'taters.

00:13:56.746 --> 00:13:58.103
I was working the garden all the time,

00:13:58.103 --> 00:14:00.313
come home, still had to go to work.

00:14:00.313 --> 00:14:02.635
And if we didn't work hard enough,

00:14:02.635 --> 00:14:04.686
he'd make us take the rock pile.

00:14:04.686 --> 00:14:06.070
Take the rock pile here,
move it over there.

00:14:06.070 --> 00:14:07.248
If he didn't like it
by the end of next week

00:14:07.248 --> 00:14:08.950
he'd make you move it back.

00:14:08.950 --> 00:14:11.922
That's how my old man was.
He was mean to all of us.

00:14:11.922 --> 00:14:14.392
He was, he was a hateful old man.

00:14:14.392 --> 00:14:17.390
I like my dad, but he was mean to my mom,

00:14:17.390 --> 00:14:19.473
but that's life, I guess.

00:14:22.339 --> 00:14:24.649
- [Interviewer] Everybody
in this county knows of you.

00:14:24.649 --> 00:14:26.407
You don't follow the rules,

00:14:26.407 --> 00:14:29.449
you seem to be like this outlaw legend

00:14:29.449 --> 00:14:31.837
that lives up on this
mountain all by himself.

00:14:31.837 --> 00:14:35.270
You've lived a life that a
lot of us fantasize about.

00:14:35.270 --> 00:14:37.711
- Ah, a crazy life, I mean,

00:14:37.711 --> 00:14:40.310
I have a lot of hand me downs.

00:14:40.310 --> 00:14:42.163
- [Interviewer] Any run ins with the law?

00:14:42.163 --> 00:14:44.917
- Oh yeah, I got away
with I've had 97 felonies

00:14:44.917 --> 00:14:45.930
and no convictions.

00:14:45.930 --> 00:14:47.721
That's what got me my five years.

00:14:47.721 --> 00:14:48.850
- [Interviewer] How many?

00:14:48.850 --> 00:14:51.573
- 97 charges, but no convictions.

00:14:51.573 --> 00:14:52.931
- [Interviewer] 97?

00:14:52.931 --> 00:14:54.240
- Yeah, charges, yeah.

00:14:54.240 --> 00:14:56.600
Felony charges and no convictions.

00:14:56.600 --> 00:14:58.762
And then finally the judge said,

00:14:58.762 --> 00:15:01.572
"Your past history is
gonna get your time."

00:15:01.572 --> 00:15:05.653
For 28 months straight before I got out.

00:15:05.653 --> 00:15:08.936
Then I got couple year sentence.

00:15:08.936 --> 00:15:10.317
When I turned over forty,

00:15:10.317 --> 00:15:12.801
that's when I said jail ain't made for me.

00:15:12.801 --> 00:15:13.962
That's when I try going straight.

00:15:13.962 --> 00:15:16.381
It's still hard. It's hard to go straight.

00:15:16.381 --> 00:15:18.638
It pays in the long run.

00:15:18.638 --> 00:15:20.130
You sleep better at night.

00:15:20.130 --> 00:15:21.050
- [Interviewer] You sleep better at night?

00:15:21.050 --> 00:15:22.014
- Yeah.
- That's good.

00:15:22.014 --> 00:15:23.764
- Yeah, being honest.

00:15:24.870 --> 00:15:26.792
All I got to do is ask people.

00:15:26.792 --> 00:15:28.012
If I don't have it, they give it to you.

00:15:28.012 --> 00:15:31.241
You know what I'm saying,
work for it, you know.

00:15:31.241 --> 00:15:33.674
As my mom always said.

00:15:33.674 --> 00:15:35.674
- [Interviewer] What are you
proud of stuff in your life?

00:15:35.674 --> 00:15:37.132
- Proud?

00:15:37.132 --> 00:15:38.809
I guess when my girl was born.

00:15:38.809 --> 00:15:42.097
That's the most proud
I've been in my life.

00:15:42.097 --> 00:15:43.678
When my girl was born.

00:15:43.678 --> 00:15:45.649
But she was born in jail.

00:15:45.649 --> 00:15:48.663
I didn't know she was mine
until she was 18 months old.

00:15:48.663 --> 00:15:51.582
But, that's one wish come true.

00:15:51.582 --> 00:15:53.624
- [Interviewer] Are you
are a religious person?

00:15:53.624 --> 00:15:55.564
- Yeah, I believe I'm Baptist, yeah.

00:15:55.564 --> 00:15:56.733
I believe in God, yeah.

00:15:56.733 --> 00:15:58.386
- [Interviewer] You ever
step foot in a church before?

00:15:58.386 --> 00:15:59.785
- Yes, yes I have.

00:15:59.785 --> 00:16:02.035
Yeah, several times.
- Yeah.

00:16:03.157 --> 00:16:04.806
- But I didn't like the preacher.

00:16:04.806 --> 00:16:09.806
He tryin' tell me somethin'
right and I done it wrong.

00:16:09.877 --> 00:16:14.040
But you know, they're evil
in church just as much

00:16:14.040 --> 00:16:15.790
as they are out here.

00:16:16.943 --> 00:16:18.642
But you can't go by that,
you've just got to go

00:16:18.642 --> 00:16:20.225
by your own belief.

00:16:22.642 --> 00:16:23.655
- [Interviewer] What do
you think of the future

00:16:23.655 --> 00:16:25.944
of life here in Appalachia for people?

00:16:25.944 --> 00:16:27.914
I mean it seems like this
lifestyle is kind of fading away.

00:16:27.914 --> 00:16:29.315
- It's gettin' rougher,
it's gettin' rougher.

00:16:29.315 --> 00:16:32.662
If you can't raise a garden
it's gonna be rough on ya.

00:16:32.662 --> 00:16:34.820
Another five years down
the road these stores

00:16:34.820 --> 00:16:36.225
are gonna go out of business.

00:16:36.225 --> 00:16:37.499
If you can't live off the land you're

00:16:37.499 --> 00:16:39.377
gonna have a hard time.

00:16:39.377 --> 00:16:41.193
It's gonna be hard. It's
gonna be rough on ya.

00:16:41.193 --> 00:16:42.617
- [Interviewer] Yeah, you're
the last of a breed I think.

00:16:42.617 --> 00:16:45.194
- [Beaver] Right, right, yup.

00:16:45.194 --> 00:16:47.026
Sure enough.

00:16:47.026 --> 00:16:50.116
Right here the animals make
livin' on the land (indistinct)

00:16:50.116 --> 00:16:52.803
You leave me here I can
live two or three on it.

00:16:52.803 --> 00:16:53.964
On my garden.

00:16:53.964 --> 00:16:55.715
- [Interviewer] Is that right?

00:16:55.715 --> 00:16:58.058
- Can goods you know.

00:16:58.058 --> 00:16:59.989
- [Interviewer] It seems
like drugs are taking over

00:16:59.989 --> 00:17:00.897
the community here.

00:17:00.897 --> 00:17:01.829
- It is, it is.

00:17:01.829 --> 00:17:02.760
- [Interviewer] It's gotten really bad

00:17:02.760 --> 00:17:03.744
in the last 20 years, right?

00:17:03.744 --> 00:17:07.628
- Yeah, I've had a lot of
good close friends leave here.

00:17:07.628 --> 00:17:10.333
That crank, and crystal meth.

00:17:10.333 --> 00:17:12.416
Heroin's now takin' over.

00:17:13.893 --> 00:17:16.945
10 years ago there was
no such thing as heroin.

00:17:16.945 --> 00:17:19.861
All there was, was maybe
a few fields of marijuana.

00:17:19.861 --> 00:17:21.528
Now it's everywhere.

00:17:24.243 --> 00:17:26.497
Dope dealers, meth dealer,

00:17:26.497 --> 00:17:28.497
it's two to one of them.

00:17:29.721 --> 00:17:30.905
- [Interviewer] What was
the worse time of your life,

00:17:30.905 --> 00:17:31.763
losing your wife?

00:17:31.763 --> 00:17:32.596
- Pardon?

00:17:32.596 --> 00:17:33.763
- [Interviewer] What was
the worse time of your life,

00:17:33.763 --> 00:17:35.171
losing your wife?

00:17:35.171 --> 00:17:36.434
- Losing my wife, yeah.

00:17:36.434 --> 00:17:38.025
Mom and dad.

00:17:38.025 --> 00:17:41.692
I lost my mom in '94,
I lost my dad in 2015.

00:17:43.148 --> 00:17:44.311
- [Interviewer] Who
were you closer to, mom?

00:17:44.311 --> 00:17:45.981
- I was close to my mom, yeah.

00:17:45.981 --> 00:17:49.231
My dad always stayed away all the time.

00:17:51.250 --> 00:17:53.377
- [Interviewer] You
ever get depressed here?

00:17:53.377 --> 00:17:54.827
- Every now and then yeah, if you don't,

00:17:54.827 --> 00:17:55.884
you know you lyin' to you.

00:17:55.884 --> 00:17:57.188
Yeah, I get depressed, yeah.

00:17:57.188 --> 00:17:58.021
- [Interviewer] Yeah.

00:17:58.021 --> 00:17:58.976
- But I'm hardly ever alone.

00:17:58.976 --> 00:17:59.816
Somebody's always here.

00:17:59.816 --> 00:18:02.006
My dog makes me company,
somebody's always here.

00:18:02.006 --> 00:18:02.957
- [Interviewer] Yeah
every time I've been here,

00:18:02.957 --> 00:18:03.798
I've been here several times

00:18:03.798 --> 00:18:05.654
and you always have people hanging out.

00:18:05.654 --> 00:18:08.606
- People live upside of me,
my family live upside of me.

00:18:08.606 --> 00:18:10.837
All that live up here is my family.

00:18:10.837 --> 00:18:13.061
Ain't no outsiders lives here.

00:18:13.061 --> 00:18:14.454
My family.

00:18:14.454 --> 00:18:18.134
It's probably been in my
family since the 1800s.

00:18:18.134 --> 00:18:19.134
Early 1800s.

00:18:21.356 --> 00:18:22.732
- [Interviewer] Your whole family's lived

00:18:22.732 --> 00:18:24.149
in Harlan county?

00:18:25.053 --> 00:18:27.062
- My old man, he was
born in this old house

00:18:27.062 --> 00:18:28.682
down here on the hill here.

00:18:28.682 --> 00:18:32.164
And he was born in '27 and
it's still standing there.

00:18:32.164 --> 00:18:33.021
- [Interviewer] How many
generations have you guys

00:18:33.021 --> 00:18:34.866
been here, you think?

00:18:34.866 --> 00:18:36.221
- Five, six.

00:18:36.221 --> 00:18:40.804
My great, great grandpa
patented this property in 1829.

00:18:42.339 --> 00:18:45.498
It's probably been in
my family ever since.

00:18:45.498 --> 00:18:47.802
My brother used to live here.

00:18:47.802 --> 00:18:51.631
My other brother was born
over there, somewhere here.

00:18:51.631 --> 00:18:54.217
And then the house got burnt, you know.

00:18:54.217 --> 00:18:55.947
- [Interviewer] Did you ever hear of what

00:18:55.947 --> 00:19:00.641
your grandfathers, great
grandparents did for money?

